  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nAn MCS, moving out of South Dakota/Minnesota into northern Iowa, \nbrought a couple of stray showers and rumbles of thunder to parts of \neastern Nebraska and western Iowa today. Behind this wave, an upper \nridge continued to shift eastward over the Central Plains. By noon, \ntemperatures had already pushed into the the low 80s, and are \nexpected to reach low to mid 90s later this afternoon. Gusty \nsouthwesterly winds brought dewpoints into the mid 60s, which will \neventually lead to heat indices in the mid to upper 90s.  \n\nPatchy cloud cover and continuing southwesterly winds will keep \ntemperatures from dropping out of the 70s overnight, before the \nthermal ridge centers over the region on Sunday. Temperatures will \nlikely bump up a few degrees from Saturday. The current forecast \ncalls for highs in the mid to upper 90s with heat indices pushing \ninto the low 100s. However, a leading shortwave, rippling through \nthe ridge ahead of an approaching trough, could throw a wrench in \nthat. A few showers and storms developing along this wave Sunday \nmorning and afternoon could take a few degrees off the high \ntemperatures, where cloud cover persists. In addition to providing \nmarginal relief from the heat, a few gusty winds or small hail could \nbe possible with any stronger storm that develops. But any severe \nweather looks unlikely with ample CAPE but weak shear. \n\nMonday and Beyond...\n\nThe upper ridge will shift west of the area Monday and flatten, \nwhile the previously mentioned trough moves northeast, dragging a \nweak \"cold\" front into the region and leaving us in relatively more \nzonal southwesterly flow through the upcoming workweek. While \ntemperatures will dip by a few degrees, highs are expected to remain \nwell above normal, in the low to mid 90s. \n\nThe southwesterly flow will invite multiple shortwaves to ripple \nthrough the region next week, bringing off and on chances for \nshowers and storms (15-30%). Confidence in the timing and extent of \nthese precipitation chances remains tied to subtle wave timing and \nmesoscale details, which should become clearer with time.",
